Alcoholic Liver Disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic heavy alcohol use, Tender hepatomegaly/jaundice, Stigmata of chronic liver disease, AST to ALT ratio > 2:1

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-002
Reason for Visit: Increasing jaundice, abdominal distension, and new-onset confusion over the past 48 hours.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute worsening of hepatic symptoms including jaundice and mild confusion (possible encephalopathy), demanding urgent face-to-face assessment to exclude fulminant hepatic failure or spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. This assessment could not safely wait until the next available in-hours appointment without risking irreversible neurological or metabolic decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My belly is completely swollen, my skin is yellow, and I keep losing my train of thought."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of chronic, heavy, and sustained alcohol consumption. Over the past week, noted progressive jaundice and scleral icterus. Abdomen has become distended and uncomfortable. Family notes a flapping tremor when the patient holds arms out and reports episodes of confusion indicating early hepatic encephalopathy.
Functional Status: Ambulating with difficulty due to abdominal distension. Requires assistance with ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to ataxia and confusion. Caregiver stress noted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 78 Kg (fluid overloaded)
General: Visibly jaundiced, chronically ill appearance. Mildly lethargic but rousable and oriented to person and place, disoriented to exact time.
Physical Exam: Scleral icterus present. Spider angiomas noted on the upper trunk. Asterixis (flapping tremor) elicited. Abdomen distended with shifting dullness indicative of ascites; tender hepatomegaly palpated. Palmar erythema present. Presentation consistent with chronic heavy alcohol use, tender hepatomegaly/jaundice, stigmata of chronic liver disease, and presumed AST to ALT ratio > 2:1.
Environmental Observations: Cluttered living space presenting a fall risk for an unsteady patient. Multiple empty alcohol containers visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Thiamine and folic acid prescribed but compliance is poor.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Alcoholic Liver Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute on Chronic)
Differentials:

Biliary Obstruction (e.g., choledocholithiasis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of ascites, spider angiomas, and asterixis which point firmly to parenchymal liver disease rather than acute mechanical obstruction.

Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever >38.0°C and severe, generalized abdominal tenderness (rebound/guarding).

Wernicke's Encephalopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of classic ophthalmoplegia and ataxia, though risk remains high.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient demonstrated acute decompensation of chronic alcoholic liver disease, evidenced by new-onset asterixis, increasing ascites, and jaundice. The combination of cognitive slowing and physical stigmata required urgent, after-hours medical evaluation to exclude life-threatening complications such as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis or severe hepatic encephalopathy. Delaying assessment until the next day posed a high risk of progression to hepatic coma or variceal bleeding. Immediate intervention and escalation to a higher level of care were clinically mandated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Thiamine 100mg. Referral to Emergency Department for management of acute alcoholic hepatitis and hepatic encephalopathy.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel confused or unsteady, immediately sit down in a safe chair or bed and alert your family member. This prevents falls and allows your caregiver to monitor you closely until medical help is secured.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently; requires inpatient stabilization.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removal of immediate trip hazards by family member.
Follow-up: Post-discharge GP follow-up within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate hospital transfer recommended. If patient refuses, call 000 for any hematemesis (vomiting blood), complete loss of consciousness, or severe acute abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute liver decompensation requires urgent hospital testing and treatment.
Clinical Course: Without complete alcohol cessation and medical management, liver failure will progress, potentially leading to coma or death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You are at risk of a serious bleed or coma. You must go to the hospital. If you vomit blood, pass black tarry stools, or become unresponsive, your caregiver must call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your liver is severely inflamed and struggling to clear toxins, which is causing your confusion and fluid build-up. The hospital will provide medications to clear these toxins and safely manage alcohol withdrawal.
Actionable Steps: Drink only clear fluids in small amounts while waiting for transport. Complete abstinence from alcohol is mandatory starting immediately; this will halt further toxic damage to your liver cells, allowing any remaining healthy tissue to function.

